WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.220
SQL injection appears when user-controlled input changes database query structure.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:14.280
In ADO.NET-style code, the primary defense is parameterized queries using DbCommand parameters.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.340
The query structure is defined by the developer, and runtime values are supplied separately.

4
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.840
This separation matters because validation may reject unusual input, but it does not make string-built SQL safe.

5
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.380
Safe construction is required at the database boundary.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.100
Entity Framework Core and other ORMs reduce many risks, but they do not eliminate review.

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:48.240
LINQ queries built from typed expressions are usually safer than hand-built query strings, but raw SQL APIs, interpolated SQL, dynamic filters, and provider-specific features still deserve attention.

8
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:55.140
Dapper can be used safely with parameter binding, yet it can also be used unsafely if values are spliced into command text.

9
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.240
The framework does not save code that bypasses its safe path.

10
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.400
Dynamic behavior needs tight constraints.

11
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:12.980
Sorting, filtering, pagination, reporting, and admin search features often tempt developers to accept field names, operators, or clauses from the client.

12
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.920
Safer designs map client choices to known columns, known properties, and known operations.

13
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:29.660
A user can request sorting by display name if display name is an approved option; the user should not provide raw SQL fragments, arbitrary property paths, or provider-specific syntax.

14
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.380
Stored procedures and least-privileged database accounts are useful, but they are not magic.

15
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:42.840
Procedures can still contain unsafe dynamic SQL, and permissions reduce blast radius rather than preventing the flaw.

16
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:57.980
A strong C# data-access pattern uses parameterized commands, safe ORM APIs, constrained dynamic options, review of raw SQL, focused tests around query-building code, and database accounts with only the permissions the service actually needs.
